{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790258195,"nonce":"TL62gVeNa9z7EuIf2KWjoxmZDJ8=","title":"Lua typer: escape and element facts read from a half-walked round","message":"Two ordering holes in crates/zyntax_lua/src/types.rs (zylua at dea6d08b; lua5.4 is the reference):\n\n1. `settle_shapes` applied the metamethod rule once, before its fixed point. A typed arithmetic handler stayed typed when an instance shape escaped only inside the fixed point (as a field of an escaping table): `pick(holder, 5).item + 10` called `__add` with 10 as a typed table and printed `table: 0x...` where Lua raises \"attempt to index a number value\". The rule also counted a class as escaping because its own `__index` names it, but only when the pass happened to reach it in that order. Now the rule runs inside the fixed point and an `__index` table escapes with the tables whose lookups reach it, not by being named there.\n2. The `ipairs` loop binding, the typed `table.sort` comparator and `table.move` read the shape's element and classes from the round being walked, where a later store is not joined yet. `for _, v in ipairs(t)` walked before `t[3] = \"x\"` bound v as Int and printed 515396075521 for \"x\". They now read the settled round, as the typer does. `table.move` from a table with a metatable moves untyped values.\n\nConformance: tables/ipairs_late_store, metatables/ipairs_index (last part).","files":null}]}